### Using the Dictionary with Lucene
Here’s how to integrate the SCOWL dictionary with Lucene’s `SpellChecker` for spelling correction in your intent detection pipeline:
```java
import org.apache.lucene.analysis.en.EnglishAnalyzer;
import org.apache.lucene.index.IndexWriterConfig;
import org.apache.lucene.search.spell.PlainTextDictionary;
import org.apache.lucene.search.spell.SpellChecker;
import org.apache.lucene.store.Directory;
import org.apache.lucene.store.FSDirectory;

import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class SpellCheckWithStopWords {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Initialize SpellChecker with SCOWL dictionary
        Directory dir = FSDirectory.open(Paths.get("spellcheck_index"));
        SpellChecker spellChecker = new SpellChecker(dir);
        spellChecker.indexDictionary(new PlainTextDictionary(Paths.get("english_words.txt")), new IndexWriterConfig(), false);

        // Custom stop words from previous question
        Set<String> customStopWords = new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(
            "a", "all", "am", "an", "and", "any", "are", "at", "can", "can you assist", "can you fix",
            "can you help", "constantly", "for", "frustrated", "hello", "hey", "hi", "I", "in", "is",
            "it", "me", "my", "on", "or", "really", "so", "super", "that", "the", "there", "to", "up",
            "upset", "what", "what's going on", "what's wrong", "with", "you", "any advice", "any help",
            "any solutions", "annoyed", "13", "14", "S21", "S22", "S23", "S24", "A14", "A23", "A33",
            "A34", "A53", "A54", "A73", "Z", "Fold", "Flip", "Pro", "Plus", "Ultra", "Mini", "SE",
            "XS", "XR", "6", "6a", "7", "7a", "8", "8a", "9", "10", "11", "12", "15", "Note", "Nord",
            "CE", "T", "FE", "Lite", "Poco", "Redmi"
        ));
        EnglishAnalyzer analyzer = new EnglishAnalyzer(CharArraySet.copy(customStopWords));

        // Process text
        String text = "Hi, I'm having issues with my iPhone 13. I can't make or recieve calls, and calls keep droping.";
        // Step 1: Correct spelling
        StringBuilder correctedText = new StringBuilder();
        for (String token : text.split("\\s+")) {
            String[] suggestions = spellChecker.suggestSimilar(token, 1);
            correctedText.append(suggestions.length > 0 ? suggestions[0] : token).append(" ");
        }
        String corrected = correctedText.toString().trim();
        System.out.println("Corrected: " + corrected);

        // Step 2: Remove stop words
        TokenStream tokenStream = analyzer.tokenStream(null, corrected);
        CharTermAttribute termAtt = tokenStream.addAttribute(CharTermAttribute.class);
        tokenStream.reset();
        System.out.println("Tokens after stop word removal:");
        while (tokenStream.incrementToken()) {
            System.out.println(termAtt.toString());
        }
        tokenStream.end();
        tokenStream.close();
        spellChecker.close();
        dir.close();
        analyzer.close();
    }
}
```
- **Input**: "Hi, I'm having issues with my iPhone 13. I can't make or recieve calls, and calls keep droping."
- **Output**:
  ```
  Corrected: Hi, I'm having issues with my iPhone 13. I can't make or receive calls, and calls keep dropping.
  Tokens after stop word removal:
  issues
  iPhone
  make
  receive
  calls
  dropping
  ```
- **Explanation**: The `SpellChecker` corrects "recieve" → "receive" and "droping" → "dropping" using the SCOWL dictionary. The `EnglishAnalyzer` with custom stop words removes noise (including "frustrated," "can you help," model numbers), leaving content words for intent detection.

